## Section 5 — Null tests

This is the make-or-break part.

A real temporal field cannot affect only the deuterium line. It should leave correlated structure.

### Null test A: metal-line coherence

If the temporal field shifts H I, it should also shift O I, Si II, C II, Fe II, etc., in related components.

Prediction:

> Apparent D/H anomalies should correlate with subtle residuals or component shifts in metal lines.

### Null test B: multi-Lyman consistency

The same temporal model must fit Ly(\alpha), Ly(\beta), Ly(\gamma), etc. simultaneously.

If it only fits one line, it fails.

### Null test C: environmental correlation

Inferred D/H should correlate with:

* (N_{\rm HI});
* metallicity;
* velocity width;
* number of fitted components;
* absorber complexity;
* proximity to galaxies;
* halo mass proxies.

Standard primordial D/H should be nearly universal after known systematics. TEP-contaminated D/H should carry environmental structure.

### Null test D: component asymmetry

If phantom D is temporal, it may prefer one side of the velocity structure. If real D, it should track H I component structure in the standard isotope-shift way.

### Null test E: blind injection

Generate synthetic spectra:

1. real D/H, no temporal shear;
2. no D/H, temporal shear;
3. mixed D/H + temporal shear.

Then test whether the fitting pipeline can recover which case is true.

## Section 6 — Bayesian evidence

Use nested sampling.

\[
\Delta\ln Z = \ln Z_{\rm TEP\ shear} - \ln Z_{\rm standard}
\]

Decision thresholds:

